On Sat, 10 Oct 2026 09:21:55 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> I want to add +archive tag in notmuch-emacs.

Setting notmuch-archive-tags to '("+archive") makes the stock "a" key
in notmuch-emacs add the tag.

> Then I want `just archive`
> to identify any messages that have the archive tag, but haven't been
> moved to the archive dir, and move them.

Posted as "[PATCH] mail: Add just archive" [1], against the mail
branch. It finds messages tagged archive that still have a file in
maildir/cur or maildir/new, moves each file unchanged into
maildir/archive/cur or maildir/archive/new, and runs notmuch new so
the index follows. Already-archived files and drafts are skipped, so
running it twice does nothing the second time. The moves stay in @ for
you to commit.

It needs no other series to apply. With "[PATCH 0/3] Archive messages
that need no answer" [2], the reindex also gives moved messages the
derived "archived" tag and takes them out of the inbox. It appends to
the Justfile, as "[PATCH] mail: Add just commit-mail" [3] does, so
whichever lands second needs a trivial rebase.

> This way I can use simple tag
> manipulation in emacs, and then have a just task to do the file
> manipulation for persistent archiving.

Yes. The "archive" tag stays a local request that is lost if the
database is rebuilt. The file under archive/ is what persists and
what everyone else sees.

